{"id":"steamy_breath","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::steamy_breath","source_path":"steamy_breath/steamy_breath.json","category":"Smoke","author":"Autodesk","description":"An aero graph producing a steamy breath style simulation","documentation_file":"steamy_breath_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Steamy Breath Graph\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses particle and aero simulations to create a steamy breath effect\n\n### Inputs and outputs \n\nInputs for this graph are the objects from which the breath will be emitted.  The output is the resulting aero steamy breath volume.\n\n### Useful information\n\n- In this graph, particles are first emitted from input geo, which in turn become sources for the aero emission. This is done to create more detail, and to leverage the advanced speed features particles have. Varying particle `speed` and `lifespan` adds even more control.  Adjusting the particle speed, lifespan, and the aero `inherit velocity` from the particles, will determine how far the breath is projected.\n\n- Scene scale is crucial, as breath is usually emitted from small volumes.  That is why the `scene units in meters` are set to 0.01 (1 cm).  \n\n- Performance is related to the particle `rate`, `fluid detail size` on the source air node, and the magnitude and mode of the aero `inherit velocity`.  \n\n### Example scene\n\n- The example scene provides context by providing a character who's mouth will exhale the steamy breath.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","particles":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"preview_geo","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"aero_volume","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"